Q: Win32 C++ SystemTimeToFileTime, FileTimeToLocalFileTime called after VariantTimeToSystemTime give incorrect results A: <p>Your <code>SYSTEMTIME</code> is local time, but <code>SystemTimeToFileTime</code> assumes the <code>SYSTEMTIME</code> you pass is UTC. Then <code>SHFormatDateTime</code> formats it as local, so you effectively apply the timezone shift once (and you see 00:00 UTC become 19:00 local the previous day, i.e., -5 hours).</p>
<p>Your second attempt goes further wrong because <code>SHFormatDateTime</code> already does the UTC→local conversion; if you pre-convert to local <code>FILETIME</code> and then call <code>SHFormatDateTime</code>, you get a double conversion (hence the extra shift to 2 PM).</p>
<p>You'll have to Convert the local <code>SYSTEMTIME</code> to UTC <code>SYSTEMTIME</code> first, then to <code>FILETIME</code>, and pass that UTC <code>FILETIME</code> to <code>SHFormatDateTime</code> without calling <code>FileTimeToLocalFileTime</code>.</p>
<pre><code>SYSTEMTIME stLocal = {};
SYSTEMTIME stUtc   = {};
FILETIME   ftUtc   = {};
VariantTimeToSystemTime(pvDateModified.date, &amp;stLocal);
// Convert LOCAL -&gt; UTC
TzSpecificLocalTimeToSystemTime(nullptr, &amp;stLocal, &amp;stUtc);
// Convert UTC SYSTEMTIME -&gt; UTC FILETIME
SystemTimeToFileTime(&amp;stUtc, &amp;ftUtc);
// SHFormatDateTime expects UTC FILETIME and will format in local time like Explorer
wchar_t datebuf[80];
DWORD flags = FDTF_NOAUTOREADINGORDER | FDTF_DEFAULT;
SHFormatDateTime(&amp;ftUtc, &amp;flags, datebuf, 80);
</code></pre>
<p>That will give you the Explorer-style local display something like 1/1/2015 12:00 AM,  instead of 12/31/2014 7:00 PM.</p>
<p>If you later discover the WPD value is already UTC some devices do that, then you should skip the TzSpecificLocalTimeToSystemTime step and pass the UTC time straight through but your observed “-5 hours” symptom matches the “local treated as UTC” mistake exactly.</p>
